  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nMostly quiet and dry conditions are expected through early\nSunday. A cold front will pass through the region early\nThursday. Through measurable precipitation chances remain low\nwith the front, generally less than 30%, given shallow boundary\nlayer moisture and weak forcing. Low-level clouds will most \nlikely make an appearance. The front should clear the Laurel \nHighlands between 8am and 11am Thursday morning. \n\nRain chances could increase late Sunday into Monday as the\nremnants of a potential tropical disturbance develops over the\nsouthwest Gulf and moves north. Should this scenario\nmaterialize, portions of the region could receive an inch to an\ninch and half of rain within an area of enhanced convergence. \nPrecipitation amounts will also heavily depend on the size and \ntrack of the low, with a potential rain shadow developing as \neasterly flow crosses over the ridges and promotes subsidence, \nlimiting rainfall amounts between Pittsburgh and the Laurel \nHighlands. \n\nThe threat for moderate to heavy rainfall should diminish late\nMonday if the system tracks into our region, becomes absorbed\ninto stronger upper-level flow over Ontario, and a trough\nbuilds over the Great Lakes.",
